-- Rollback for 0053_autopilot_entitlement_dismiss_reason.sql
--
-- Postgres has no `ALTER TYPE ... DROP VALUE`, so the 'entitlement'
-- label stays in the type — additive and inert: no reader switches
-- exhaustively on dismiss_reason at the DB boundary, and Activity's
-- review-outcome surfaces allowlist ('user','protected') explicitly,
-- so older code simply never sees it (the 0045 precedent).
--
-- The rows the label was written to are deliberately NOT restored.
-- An earlier draft flipped every entitlement-dismissed match back to
-- (resolution='approved', intent_applied=false) — a bit-exact restore
-- of the pre-demotion tuple, and exactly the hazard D251's demotion
-- exists to kill: on any workspace that holds (or later regains)
-- `autopilot-active`, the next action sweep would EXECUTE those
-- months-stale matches unattended, and the reconciliation sweep only
-- re-dismisses under-entitled tiers, so an entitled workspace has no
-- safety net (Codex stop-review 2026-08-04). A migration rollback
-- reverts schema; it must not re-arm mail-moving side effects.
--
-- If a specific workspace's demotion genuinely needs reversing, that
-- is a support operation: verify the affected rows by hand, confirm
-- with the customer, and update only those ids.
SELECT 1;
